Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Scratch work of Author", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.971382027808633*^9, 3.971382031684561*^9}, 
   3.9716353846950455`*^9},
 FontSize->24,ExpressionUUID->"6e2f540a-b152-4261-b990-757173d468c9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["NOTES", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.972494740406041*^9, 3.972494744544758*^9}, {
  3.973100741003458*^9, 3.973100744361723*^9}, {3.9742780753702545`*^9, 
  3.974278077773403*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5cf8a0dc-9575-b340-a49e-\
0ed723480ba8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Scratch work", "Title",
  FontSize->24],
 "\n",
 StyleBox["Patrick L. Nash, Ph.D.",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["\n",
  FontSize->18,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["(c) 2022, under GPL\nProfessor, UTSA Physics and Astronomy, \
Retired (UTSA)\nPatrick299Nash  at    gmail   ...\nDo not read if you are \
irascible; I apologize for typos.\ngit clone \
https://github.com/43d168f3e/Eternal-DEFLATION-Inflation.git\n\
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]git@github.com:43d168f3e/Eternal-DEFLATION-Inflation.\
git\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]\n\n\nEver wonder why there are  3 space \
dimensions and  1  time dimension?  \nEver wonder if   Nature  loves  the \
norm  relation \:2016XY\:2016 = \:2016X\:2016\:2016Y\:2016, for  X, Y \
\[Element] Nature?\n\nWell, Hurwitz\[CloseCurlyQuote]s theorem  says  that  \
there  are  only  4 normed division algebras (\:2016XY\:2016=\:2016X\:2016\
\:2016Y\:2016) over the real numbers, which, up to isomorphism,  are   the  \
1-dim (over \[DoubleStruckCapitalR]) real numbers  \
\[DoubleStruckCapitalR],the   2-dim  complex numbers,the   4-dim   \
quaternions, and the  8-dim   octonions.\n\nSince  this   is   the   age  of  \
  the  Xtreme,   let\[CloseCurlyQuote]s   go   with   the    8-dim   \
octonions.    Actually   the   split   octonions, in this notebook,    with   \
4   space    dimensions   and   4   time   dimensions.\n\nThe origin of this \
calculation has its roots in \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]A Remarkable \
Representation of the 3+2 de Sitter Group\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] by \
P.A.M.Dirac, J.Math.Phys.4,901\[Dash]909 (1963). \n\nHere, Dirac\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s gamma matrices have been extended into  this paper\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s  real tau8  8\[Cross]8 and  tau16 = \[Tau]   16\[Cross]16 \
matrices, in order to simply incorporate and \ncouple the ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["WAVE FUNCTION OF the UNIVERSE",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.93, 0.27, 0.27]],
 StyleBox[" to Gravity\nvia a term resembling\n\[Psi]^\[Dagger]  (mass matrix \
for universe) \[Psi] :   \n(mass matrix for universe) \[Tilde]  (\[Tau]0 \
\[Tau]1 \[Tau]2 \[Tau]3 )(\[Tau]5 \[Tau]6 \[Tau]7), \n[ \[Tau]5 \[Tau]6 \
\[Tau]7 breaks \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["x5, x6, x7",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.93, 0.27, 0.27]],
 StyleBox["\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]  Lorentz invariance ].\n\nHere we use ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["spacetime coordinates",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.4, 0.6, 1]],
 StyleBox[" that are selected so that we are somewhat consistent with the \n",
  
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["xact libraries",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.14, 0.8, 0.14]],
 StyleBox[",\n(which we employ, in other included notebooks, in order to \
calculate the Einstein-Lovelock tensors):\n",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["Cartesian coordinates:\n",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.4, 0.6, 1],
  Background->None],
 StyleBox["  x0 = hidden space (a small circle); ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["x1, x2, x3",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.93, 0.27, 0.27]],
 StyleBox["  are the usual 3-space coords; \n",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["x4",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.93, 0.27, 0.27]],
 StyleBox[" = time coord, \nand  ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["x5, x6, x7",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.93, 0.27, 0.27]],
 StyleBox["= superluminal ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["deflating",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.93, 0.27, 0.27]],
 StyleBox[" time coords}.\n\n\nThe split Octonion   algebra   carries   basic \
   8-component   representations of   ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   OverscriptBox[
    RowBox[{"SO", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"4", ",", 
       RowBox[{"4", ";", " ", "\[DoubleStruckCapitalR]"}]}], ")"}]}], "_"], 
   TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"2d853c2a-fe53-f04b-93d3-c61608da9fb6"],
 StyleBox["  (left-spinor,   right-spinor   and   vector),   which   are   \
equivalent  [Cartan\[CloseCurlyQuote]s   triality].\n\nHere  we  find a \
solution of the Einstein-Lovelock vacuum field equations on a spacetime M8 ,  \
whose tangent bundle has\n ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   OverscriptBox[
    RowBox[{"SO", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"4", ",", 
       RowBox[{"4", ";", " ", "\[DoubleStruckCapitalR]"}]}], ")"}]}], "_"], 
   TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"9365dd67-0f7e-214e-b58e-f8b919033f98"],
 StyleBox[" \[TildeTilde] Spin(4,4;\[DoubleStruckCapitalR])\nas iso group,  \
and in which\n3 of the 4 space dimensions ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["superluminally INFLATE",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.4, 0.6, 1]],
 StyleBox[",\n3 of the 4 time dimensions ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["superluminally DEFLATE",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.93, 0.27, 0.27]],
 StyleBox[" (no problems with causality in the surviving time dimension, \
although  I am sure that the 3 deflated time dimensions are interesting),\n\
and the 4th space dim, x0,  curls up into a ring (",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["hence a particle whose wave function penetrates  this ring \
acquires a mass contribution",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.67, 0.54, 0.42]],
 StyleBox[").\n ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["TODO: prove that  ",
  FontSize->36,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox[" ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["Universe(s) of  masses  \[PlusMinus]M are created in pairs!",
  FontSize->36,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.93, 0.27, 0.27]],
 StyleBox["\n\n\nIn passing we remark that an analogous construction may be \
defined on  an octonion space of ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["one time dimension and seven space dimensions",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.4, 0.6, 1]],
 StyleBox[", if the reader is allergic to multiple time dimensions (and \
employ the octonions rather than the split-octonions).  \n\n",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 StyleBox["Unsolved problem 1",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.4, 0.6, 1]],
 StyleBox[" (of many): All particles are initially massless in the standard \
model, due to gauge invariance under the symmetry group\nSU(3)  \
\[CircleTimes]   ",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox[
    RowBox[{"SU", "(", "2", ")"}], "L"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f50e40fd-de7d-3348-961c-24c429fa4939"],
 "  \[CircleTimes] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{" ", 
    SubscriptBox[
     RowBox[{"U", "(", "1", ")"}], "Y"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9b0eca42-9315-a745-91a8-680e0ddfbb4d"],
 StyleBox[";  \nThe photon should remain massless after the Higgs field \
acquires a vacuum expectation value (spontaneous symmetry breaking).\n\n\n\
This notebook is distributed as free software under the GNU General Public \
License.They are distributed in the hope that they will  be useful,but \
WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY;without even the implied warranty of MERCHANTABILITY or \
FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.See the GNU GPL for more details.\n\nPlease  \
cite  this  work  and this web page if you use it.\n\n",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 Cell[BoxData[{
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"The", " ", "Einstein"}], "-", 
     RowBox[{
     "Lovelock", "  ", "vacuum", "  ", "field", "  ", "equations", "  ", 
      "are"}]}], " "}],
   FontSize->16,
   FontColor->GrayLevel[0]], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SubsuperscriptBox["A", "a", "b"], "  ", "=", "  ", "0"}], ",", " ", 
     "where"}], " "}],
   FontSize->16,
   FontColor->GrayLevel[0]], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
